"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,351\nEpisode 3\n\n3\n00:00:07,431 --> 00:00:10,128\nLarks, owls and 8 a.m.\n\n4\n00:00:12,928 --> 00:00:14,636\nSome people wake up sharp.\n\n5\n00:00:14,925 --> 00:00:16,894\nOthers hit their stride late at night.\n\n6\n00:00:17,292 --> 00:00:19,382\nResearchers call this your chronotype: lark,\n\n7\n00:00:19,656 --> 00:00:22,560\nowl, or somewhere in between.\n\n8\n00:00:22,903 --> 00:00:24,776\nAnd among university students,\n\n9\n00:00:24,796 --> 00:00:26,173\nowls are common.\n\n10\n00:00:26,343 --> 00:00:28,724\nIn one pool of university students,\n\n11\n00:00:28,992 --> 00:00:31,260\nthirty one percent were evening types,\n\n12\n00:00:31,435 --> 00:00:34,149\nand fewer than eight percent were morning types.\n\n13\n00:00:34,574 --> 00:00:35,812\nMost were in the middle,\n\n14\n00:00:35,855 --> 00:00:37,446\nbut even they leaned late.\n\n15\n00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:40,145\nAt the National University of Singapore,\n\n16\n00:00:40,449 --> 00:00:46,841\nresearchers used Wi-Fi logs to estimate lecture attendance for more than twenty three thousand students.\n\n17\n00:00:47,245 --> 00:00:49,625\nAttendance at eight a.m.\n\n18\n00:00:50,033 --> 00:00:54,259\nclasses was about ten percentage points lower than at later times.\n\n19\n00:00:54,429 --> 00:01:00,650\nWatch data from a hundred and eighty one students showed about an hour less sleep before an early class,\n\n20\n00:01:00,954 --> 00:01:03,242\nbecause they woke earlier than usual.\n\n21\n00:01:03,412 --> 00:01:06,404\nAnd across nearly thirty four thousand students,\n\n22\n00:01:06,697 --> 00:01:09,436\nthe more days a week they had morning classes,\n\n23\n00:01:09,746 --> 00:01:12,742\nthe lower their grade point average tended to be.\n\n24\n00:01:12,912 --> 00:01:14,905\nTiming hits owls hardest.\n\n25\n00:01:15,288 --> 00:01:16,747\nIn Dutch high schools,\n\n26\n00:01:16,883 --> 00:01:20,245\nlate chronotypes did worse on early morning exams,\n\n27\n00:01:20,512 --> 00:01:23,599\nbut in the early afternoon the gap was not significant.\n\n28\n00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:30,699\nLab studies call this the synchrony effect: demanding tasks tend to go best at your own peak time.\n\n29\n00:01:30,869 --> 00:01:31,845\nSome caution.\n\n30\n00:01:32,246 --> 00:01:34,276\nThese are mostly correlations.\n\n31\n00:01:34,590 --> 00:01:36,408\nIn one American university,\n\n32\n00:01:36,526 --> 00:01:41,958\nmorning types still had slightly better grades even after class times were taken into account.\n\n33\n00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:44,087\nAnd chronotype can shift,\n\n34\n00:01:44,316 --> 00:01:45,817\neven within a semester.\n\n35\n00:01:45,987 --> 00:01:46,816\nTry this.\n\n36\n00:01:47,137 --> 00:01:48,588\nNotice when you feel sharpest,\n\n37\n00:01:48,608 --> 00:01:50,388\nand put your hardest study there.\n\n38\n00:01:50,739 --> 00:01:51,987\nIf you can choose,\n\n39\n00:01:52,131 --> 00:01:54,350\navoid stacking early classes.\n\n40\n00:01:54,642 --> 00:01:58,938\nIf you can't, keep your wake time steady so the early start hurts less.\n\n41\n00:01:59,211 --> 00:02:02,252\nAnd save routine tasks for your low hours.\n\n42\n00:02:02,422 --> 00:02:03,322\nKnow your clock.\n\n43\n00:02:03,342 --> 00:02:04,544\nThen work with it.\n\n"
